oo including wastewater sequencing uh  we can train uh you know students and  researchers to be able to perform the  same and ask questions of the publicly  available data and so  on the learn portal that you have just  seen  you will see  this kind of an overview that gives the  basics of multiple sequence alignment as  well as the ability to ask questions  about origins of source code 2 or how  the new variants are emerging and how  they might affect the future of this  pandemic so at this point i briefly want  to introduce another element that i  think is critical for those students  that are serious about their interest in  bioinformatics  and what we see  time and time again is that  students going through short-term  training  are unable to capture a lot of the  aspects  of  bioinformatics the application of  bioinformatics but also the ability to  independently  learn and continue to apply  bioinformatics once those resources are  unavailable  and so for that reason we wanted to make  sure that many of the same methods  that are available through such training  could be taken away with the researcher  or the student after the program  completion and they're able to replicate  and even use those in their um  you know reporting or  ability to analyze data and so i briefly  want to show you a course that is  designed to kind of give you a broad  understanding of how this portal works  and that will be introduction to  bioinformatics so if you go to  introduction to bioinformatics  um  sorry  bytes and molecules is what i wanted to  say okay so bytes and molecules  the bites and molecules course  uh really covers many of the aspects of  what we just discussed for someone who  is a beginner and has minimum or no  background in bioinformatics wants to  understand the terminology that is used  in such training and wants to be  prepared with some of the technical  skills that are going to be needed  and one of the courses that will be  available in this  section is an introduction to coding  so coding  is an important part of a  bioinformatician's  you know day-to-day life and activity  but it is often seen as an exclusive  skill set that only bioinformaticians  and engineers really need to have but as  we all know in many aspects of research  just to produce the picture that you  need the figure  maybe some simple data analysis with  some statistical properties it is always  useful to be able to  know that piece of code that if you know  how to run it you know how to give it  the right input you can make it produce  the right output that you want and that  is exactly the objective of some of this  very basic introductory training that we  offer in this section called  introduction to bioinformatics languages  and this one is an example using r  so here you will find some very basic  things that you need to understand about  data how it is stored and how it is used  in the coding environment  but we don't have to set up a whole  environment on your own system to be  able to run and produce even the basic  plots that you might want to use in your  research publications or exploratory  analysis of a data set that could be  very informative  so we offer here  inside the actual lesson  a console that you can run  and get result  right here  and as you can see immediately if i just  run this piece of code i am getting an  error  and the reason is because obviously this  path to the file does not work right and  so one of the basic challenges that all  of us probably faced when we started  learning how to code i can learn how to  write hello world but how do i actually  make this code work for my data right  and that's exactly where we start so  just by copying this link  and replacing it right here in the  console  i can run the code  and i can get some results right and so  right here  you can see that  i can check the results  and now i have a successful result right  so i need to learn about some of the  syntax just some basics of writing how  to code i need to understand the logic  of how to find the right data and give  it the right link and then once i do  this console will check for me whether  i'm doing the right thing and now i can  extend this to more advanced  applications like running a script to  prepare a box plot or maybe  changing the way that this figure looks  right and eventually i will be  introduced to a an assignment and that  assignment will be right writing a  complete piece of code right so if i  know that i can complete this assignment  i'm not only following a step-by-step  tutorial but i'm in fact able to apply  what i have learned and now i can copy  this  and use it on my system to be able to  run that every time i face a result so  what kinds of activities are possible  for this type of  learning  one of the training that we do in terms  of source code 2 is performing multiple  sequence alignment and phylodynamics of  the pandemic outbreak in different  regions to really understand the  emerging variants or clades that have  been reported throughout the course of  the pandemic so as you can see the logic  of analysis is first done on the  platform  and then  we actually go through a series of steps  to generate a variant report and this is  a report not unlike what people use when  they use clinical reporting or or when  that reporting is being submitted to  you know maybe public health  institutions to understand whether there  is a particular variant of concern  or maybe there are some number of  samples that you know indicate that  there's a spread of that bearing of  concern so we go through preparing such  a report  in r how to install all of the different  tools that are needed on your own system  and then how to actually run that with  the specific types of variants that you  might be interested in so it really  provides an ability to apply the logic  try it on publicly available data and  potentially then  apply the same to novel data that is  being collected now we don't stay at the  sequence level we also try to  incorporate the association between  sequence and the structure so we look at  the spiked protein we understand the  spike protein and even map the mutations  and understand the relationship between  the proteins so that we understand how  they are interacting and as a result  understand the significance of some of  those variants and all of that is done  in under a month-long training program  now another big part of what we do  going beyond the traditional  bioinformatics where we deal a lot with  sequence data and visualization is  actually learn about data science and  for data science we have this very basic  introduction that i just showed you in r  and python but we also want to be able  to apply some of those methods of  exploratory data analysis statistical  analysis machine learning in a more  advanced environment and so we created  this program to offer  you know greater insights into what is  possible  just knowing some of those basics  again this is project oriented so we  start with  seemingly simple challenges for example  in the context of precision medicine can  we identify a patient that is likely to  respond or not respond to a particular  type of  medication  and then we use that information to be  able to explain some of the additional  downstream requirements for dealing with  bigger data how you can use different  packages how you can produce advanced  visualizations how you can apply similar  approaches to different types of data be  it genomic metagenomic transcriptomic  and others and eventually how to also  apply this using machine learning  you know advanced statistical analysis  and an understanding of how to apply  these skills to bigger data sets  now we've recently completed a program  like this that was you you know all of  it was designed around python  from very basics  to more and more advanced types of  applications where we also highlighted  the significance of an online  cloud-based environment to be able to  write reproducible code and i think  those are two very important trends that  students need to be introduced to as  they move into the application of  bioinformatics or data science to  particular types of research not only do  we need to be able to extract meaningful  insights we also need to know how we got  those insights and provide an  environment where others can easily  reproduce the same code that we write  which means that the code needs to be  readable which means that we need to  explain what we're doing and why we're  doing that and also highlight the  results  with a piece of code that somebody can  take and  run and and get the exact same thing and  so there we cover things like uh you  know  dimensionality reduction uh ordination  and embedding  we look at different types of  classification models including the  understanding of how those models work  and how principles of those  logistic regression for example methods  are used in  more advanced methods like deep learning  and also  connect that with again practical  examples and those practical examples  could be used for uh  projects that have made a significant  impact so after we cover this domain of  machine learning we actually take a look  at several different publications where  all of the methods that we cover are  explained in the abstract itself and the  student can really see and appreciate  how the  specific outcome from some research  really affected  the industry or even the clinical use of  those methods so for example here  modeling precision treatment of breast  cancer is a project that we use to  illustrate individualized and  personalized treatment as well as  predictive modeling to understand  potential response that does not require  data from that patient themselves over a  course of different types of treatments  right which is obviously not possible  and i think very relevant to the medical  field which is moving further and  further into the adoption of such tools  and the precision treatment of  different types of complex diseases  now  beyond this training and i'll i'll be  done here in a couple of minutes we  offer  to use the same elements that i've just  described the t by info platform for no  code bioinformatics the coding elements  that you can embed right into your  coursework your expertise in projects by  illustrating a specific type of a method  in application to a data set that you  understand and might have published  before  as a resource for researchers and  faculty that would like to incorporate  their own training and make it available  to the nepu network so one of the ideas  is to  identify gaps  for example if you take an institution  that might not have a full  bioinformatics  core  and they might not have the  you know facilities to be able to  support bioinformatics and computational  biology research  we would like to match them with another  institution that might have those  capabilities but might be lacking  another capability that that university  does have and so by linking those we're  able to address the gap across the whole  network  one example that i would like to show is  a course that we designed together with  dr sona vasudevan  she is the program director at the  georgetown university medical center for  a program called systems medicine and  her special is her specialty is in  bioinformatics using 3d protein  structures and really uh kind of looking  at the relationship between sequence and  structure and so we worked with her to  create a summer course where her  students and students from other uh  backgrounds could join and and learn the  basics of genomics in the virtual lab  we also worked with researchers that  specialize in oncology  developing a program on precision  oncology and  we also kind of design these together in  collaboration with other researchers and  faculty and we are hoping to expand our  network and really uh kind of build the  